2.3 Non-Inertial Frames and Effective Forces






TipTranslational Non-inertial Reference Frame





Consider a non-inertial reference frame \(S'\) that translates relative to an inertial frame \(S\) with an acceleration \(\vec{a}_0\). The transformation of acceleration between these frames is given by
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{a} = \vec{a}' + \vec{a}_0.
\end{aligned}
\]
Applying Newton’s Second Law in the inertial frame \(S\), we obtain
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{F} = m\vec{a} = m(\vec{a}' + \vec{a}_0).
\end{aligned}
\]
Rearranging this equation, we can express the equation of motion in the non-inertial frame \(S'\) as
\[
\begin{aligned}
m\vec{a}' = \vec{F} + \vec{F}_a,
\end{aligned}
\]
where
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{F}_a = -m\vec{a}_0
\end{aligned}
\]
is the effective force, or fictitious force, in the non-inertial frame \(S'\).









TipRotational Non-inertial Reference Frame






Coordinate transformations:

\[
\begin{aligned}
r = r', \quad \dot{r} = \dot{r}', \quad \ddot{r} = \ddot{r}', \\
\theta = \theta' + \omega t, \quad \dot{\theta} = \dot{\theta}' + \omega, \quad \ddot{\theta} = \ddot{\theta}'.
\end{aligned}
\]

Unit vector transformations:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\hat{r} = \hat{r}', \\
\hat{\theta} = \hat{\theta}'.
\end{aligned}
\]
In the inertial frame \(S\), the position vector is \(\vec{r} = r \hat{r}\). Taking the time derivative in the inertial frame:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{v} = \frac{d\vec{r}}{dt} = \dot{r} \hat{r} + r \dot{\hat{r}}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Using the relation for the derivative of a unit vector in polar coordinates, \(\dot{\hat{r}} = \dot{\theta} \hat{\theta}\). Substituting \(\dot{\theta} = \dot{\theta}' + \omega\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{v} &= \dot{r} \hat{r} + r(\dot{\theta}' + \omega) \hat{\theta} = (\dot{r} \hat{r} + r \dot{\theta}' \hat{\theta}) + r \omega \hat{\theta}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Since \(\vec{v}' = \dot{r}' \hat{r}' + r' \dot{\theta}' \hat{\theta}'\) and \(\vec{\omega} \times \vec{r} =  r \omega \hat{\theta}\), we obtain:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{v} = \vec{v}' + \vec{\omega} \times \vec{r}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then \[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{a}_r &= (\ddot{r} - r\dot{\theta}^2)\hat{r} = [\ddot{r}' - r'(\dot{\theta}' + \omega)^2]\hat{r}' = \vec{a}'_{r'} - 2\vec{v}'_{\theta'} \times \vec{\omega} - \omega^2\vec{r}', \\
\vec{a}_\theta &= (r\ddot{\theta} + 2\dot{r}\dot{\theta})\hat{\theta} = [r'\ddot{\theta}' + 2\dot{r}'(\dot{\theta}' + \omega)]\hat{\theta}' = \vec{a}'_{\theta'} - 2\vec{v}'_{r'} \times \vec{\omega}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Combining the last two equations, we obtain:
\[
\begin{aligned}
m(\vec{a}'_{r'} + \vec{a}'_{\theta'}) &= m(\vec{a}_r + \vec{a}_\theta) + m\omega^2\vec{r}' + 2m(\vec{v}'_{r'} + \vec{v}'_{\theta'}) \times \vec{\omega}, \\
\vec{F}_{\text{eff}} &= \vec{F}_{\text{real}} + \vec{F}_{\text{c}} + \vec{F}_{\text{cor}}.
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(\vec{F}_{\text{c}}\) and \(\vec{F}_{\text{cor}}\) are the centrifugal force and Coriolis force, respectively:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\vec{F}_{\text{c}} &= m\omega^2\vec{r}', \\
\vec{F}_{\text{cor}} &= 2m(\vec{v}'_{r'} + \vec{v}'_{\theta'}) \times \vec{\omega} = 2m\vec{v}' \times \vec{\omega}.
\end{aligned}
\]

