TipRiemann Integral















TipIntegral on a Rectangular Region





A subset of \(\mathbb{R}^m\) of the form \[
\begin{aligned}
R &= \{(x^1, x^2, \dots, x^m) \in \mathbb{R}^m \mid a^k \le x^k \le b^k, k=1, 2, \dots, m\} \\
&= [a^1, b^1] \times [a^2, b^2] \times \dots \times [a^m, b^m]
\end{aligned}
\] is called an \(m\)-dimensional rectangle in \(\mathbb{R}^m\), denoted as \[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_m(R) := (b^1 - a^1)(b^2 - a^2) \dots (b^m - a^m)
\end{aligned}
\] which is called the volume of \(R\). When it does not lead to confusion, we also denote \(\mu(R) = \mu_m(R)\).
A collection \(\{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) is called a partition \(\mathcal{P}\) of the rectangle \(R\) if each \(R_k\) is an \(m\)-dimensional rectangle in \(\mathbb{R}^m\), and \[
\begin{aligned}
\bigcup_{k=1}^p R_k = R, \quad \text{int} R_i \cap \text{int} R_j = \emptyset \quad (\text{for any } 1 \le i &lt; j \le p).
\end{aligned}
\]
Given a function \(f: R \to \mathbb{R}\), for a partition \(\mathcal{P} = \{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) of the rectangle \(R\) and a corresponding set of tagged points \(\xi = \{\xi_k\}_{k=1}^p\) (where each \(\xi_k \in R_k\)), let \[
\begin{aligned}
S(f, \mathcal{P}, \xi) = \sum_{k=1}^p f(\xi_k) \mu(R_k)
\end{aligned}
\] This is called the Riemann sum of \(f\) on \(R\) with respect to the partition \(\mathcal{P}\) and the set of tagged points \(\xi\).
We say that \(f\) is Riemann integrable on \(R\) if there exists \(I \in \mathbb{R}\) such that for any \(\epsilon &gt; 0\), there exists \(\delta &gt; 0\) such that for any partition \(\mathcal{P}\) of \(R\), as long as \[
\begin{aligned}
\|\mathcal{P}\| = \max_{1 \le k \le p} \left( \sup_{x,y \in R_k} \|x - y\| \right) &lt; \delta
\end{aligned}
\] we have \(|S(f, \mathcal{P}, \xi) - I| &lt; \epsilon\) for any set of tagged points \(\xi\). At this time, we denote \[
\begin{aligned}
I = \int_R f(x) d\mu = \int_R f(x) dx = \int_R f(x) dx_1 \dots dx_m,
\end{aligned}
\] and call it the Riemann integral of \(f\) on \(R\).









TipDarboux Integral





Let \(f: R \to \mathbb{R}\) be a bounded function on a rectangle \(R\), and \(\mathcal{P} = \{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) be a partition of \(R\). Let \[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) = \sum_{k=1}^p \sup_{x \in R_k} f(x) \mu(R_k), \quad \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) = \sum_{k=1}^p \inf_{x \in R_k} f(x) \mu(R_k),
\end{aligned}
\] which are called the Darboux upper sum and Darboux lower sum of \(f\) on \(R\) with respect to the partition \(\mathcal{P}\), respectively.
A partition \(\mathcal{Q} = \{\tilde{R}_j\}_{j=1}^q\) of \(R\) is called a refinement of \(\mathcal{P} = \{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) if each rectangle \(R_k\) in \(\mathcal{P}\) is a union of some rectangles in \(\mathcal{Q}\).
It is easy to see that for any two partitions \(\mathcal{P}_1, \mathcal{P}_2\) of the rectangle \(R\), there exists a common refinement denoted by \(\mathcal{P}_1 \lor \mathcal{P}_2\), and \[
\begin{aligned}
\underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_1) \le \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_1 \lor \mathcal{P}_2) \le \overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_1 \lor \mathcal{P}_2) \le \overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_2).
\end{aligned}
\]
Let \(\mathfrak{P}\) be the set of all possible partitions of the rectangle \(R\). We call \[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{S}(f) = \inf_{\mathcal{P} \in \mathfrak{P}} \overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}), \quad \underline{S}(f) = \sup_{\mathcal{P} \in \mathfrak{P}} \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P})
\end{aligned}
\] the Darboux upper integral and Darboux lower integral of \(f\) on \(R\), respectively.
If \(\overline{S}(f) = \underline{S}(f)\), then \(f\) is said to be Darboux integrable on \(R\). Their common value is denoted as \[
\begin{aligned}
I = \int_R f(x) d\mu = \int_R f(x) dx = \int_R f(x) dx_1 \dots dx_m
\end{aligned}
\] and is called the Darboux integral of \(f\) on \(R\).




Theorem 5.1 The following conclusions are equivalent:

\(f\) is Riemann integrable on the rectangle \(R\);
\(f\) is bounded and Darboux integrable on the rectangle \(R\);
\(f\) is bounded on the rectangle \(R\), and the set of discontinuities of \(f\) in \(R\) is a set of volume zero (measure zero);
For any \(\epsilon &gt; 0\), there exists a partition \(\mathcal{P}\) of the rectangle \(R\) such that \[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) - \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) &lt; \epsilon.
\end{aligned}
\]

If \(f\) is Riemann (Darboux) integrable on \(R\), then the Riemann integral of \(f\) is equal to the Darboux integral of \(f\).


Theorem 5.2 Let \(R \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) be an \(m\)-dimensional rectangle. Let \(\mathscr{R}(R)\) be the set of all Riemann integrable functions on \(R\), and \(\mathscr{C}(R)\) be the set of all continuous functions on \(R\). Then:

(Linearity) \(\mathscr{R}(R)\) is a linear space, and \(\mathscr{C}(R)\) is a linear subspace of \(\mathscr{R}(R)\). Furthermore, for any \(f, g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\) and any \(\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R}\): \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_R (\alpha f(x) + \beta g(x)) d\mu = \alpha \int_R f(x) d\mu + \beta \int_R g(x) d\mu;
\end{aligned}
\]
(Monotonicity) If \(f, g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\) satisfy \(f(x) \le g(x)\) for all \(x \in R\), then: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_R f(x) d\mu \le \int_R g(x) d\mu;
\end{aligned}
\]
\(f \in \mathscr{R}(R)\) if and only if \(f_+, f_- \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), where: \[
\begin{aligned}
f_+(x) &= \max\{f(x), 0\} = \frac{|f(x)| + f(x)}{2}, \\
f_-(x) &= \max\{-f(x), 0\} = \frac{|f(x)| - f(x)}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\] Thus \(f = f_+ - f_-\) and \(|f| = f_+ + f_-\). Furthermore: \[
\begin{aligned}
\left| \int_R f(x) d\mu \right| \le \int_R |f(x)| d\mu;
\end{aligned}
\]
(Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality) For any \(f, g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), it follows that \(fg \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), and: \[
\begin{aligned}
\left| \int_R f(x)g(x) d\mu \right| \le \sqrt{\int_R |f(x)|^2 d\mu \cdot \int_R |g(x)|^2 d\mu};
\end{aligned}
\]
(Mean Value Theorem for Integrals) If \(f \in \mathscr{C}(R)\), \(g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), and \(g(x) \ge 0\) for all \(x \in R\), then there exists \(\eta \in R\) such that:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_R f(x)g(x) d\mu = f(\eta) \int_R g(x) d\mu.
\end{aligned}
\]

TipRiemann Integral















TipIntegral on a Rectangular Region





A subset of \(\mathbb{R}^m\) of the form \[
\begin{aligned}
R &= \{(x^1, x^2, \dots, x^m) \in \mathbb{R}^m \mid a^k \le x^k \le b^k, k=1, 2, \dots, m\} \\
&= [a^1, b^1] \times [a^2, b^2] \times \dots \times [a^m, b^m]
\end{aligned}
\] is called an \(m\)-dimensional rectangle in \(\mathbb{R}^m\), denoted as \[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_m(R) := (b^1 - a^1)(b^2 - a^2) \dots (b^m - a^m)
\end{aligned}
\] which is called the volume of \(R\). When it does not lead to confusion, we also denote \(\mu(R) = \mu_m(R)\).
A collection \(\{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) is called a partition \(\mathcal{P}\) of the rectangle \(R\) if each \(R_k\) is an \(m\)-dimensional rectangle in \(\mathbb{R}^m\), and \[
\begin{aligned}
\bigcup_{k=1}^p R_k = R, \quad \text{int} R_i \cap \text{int} R_j = \emptyset \quad (\text{for any } 1 \le i &lt; j \le p).
\end{aligned}
\]
Given a function \(f: R \to \mathbb{R}\), for a partition \(\mathcal{P} = \{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) of the rectangle \(R\) and a corresponding set of tagged points \(\xi = \{\xi_k\}_{k=1}^p\) (where each \(\xi_k \in R_k\)), let \[
\begin{aligned}
S(f, \mathcal{P}, \xi) = \sum_{k=1}^p f(\xi_k) \mu(R_k)
\end{aligned}
\] This is called the Riemann sum of \(f\) on \(R\) with respect to the partition \(\mathcal{P}\) and the set of tagged points \(\xi\).
We say that \(f\) is Riemann integrable on \(R\) if there exists \(I \in \mathbb{R}\) such that for any \(\epsilon &gt; 0\), there exists \(\delta &gt; 0\) such that for any partition \(\mathcal{P}\) of \(R\), as long as \[
\begin{aligned}
\|\mathcal{P}\| = \max_{1 \le k \le p} \left( \sup_{x,y \in R_k} \|x - y\| \right) &lt; \delta
\end{aligned}
\] we have \(|S(f, \mathcal{P}, \xi) - I| &lt; \epsilon\) for any set of tagged points \(\xi\). At this time, we denote \[
\begin{aligned}
I = \int_R f(x) d\mu = \int_R f(x) dx = \int_R f(x) dx_1 \dots dx_m,
\end{aligned}
\] and call it the Riemann integral of \(f\) on \(R\).









TipDarboux Integral





Let \(f: R \to \mathbb{R}\) be a bounded function on a rectangle \(R\), and \(\mathcal{P} = \{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) be a partition of \(R\). Let \[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) = \sum_{k=1}^p \sup_{x \in R_k} f(x) \mu(R_k), \quad \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) = \sum_{k=1}^p \inf_{x \in R_k} f(x) \mu(R_k),
\end{aligned}
\] which are called the Darboux upper sum and Darboux lower sum of \(f\) on \(R\) with respect to the partition \(\mathcal{P}\), respectively.
A partition \(\mathcal{Q} = \{\tilde{R}_j\}_{j=1}^q\) of \(R\) is called a refinement of \(\mathcal{P} = \{R_k\}_{k=1}^p\) if each rectangle \(R_k\) in \(\mathcal{P}\) is a union of some rectangles in \(\mathcal{Q}\).
It is easy to see that for any two partitions \(\mathcal{P}_1, \mathcal{P}_2\) of the rectangle \(R\), there exists a common refinement denoted by \(\mathcal{P}_1 \lor \mathcal{P}_2\), and \[
\begin{aligned}
\underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_1) \le \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_1 \lor \mathcal{P}_2) \le \overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_1 \lor \mathcal{P}_2) \le \overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}_2).
\end{aligned}
\]
Let \(\mathfrak{P}\) be the set of all possible partitions of the rectangle \(R\). We call \[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{S}(f) = \inf_{\mathcal{P} \in \mathfrak{P}} \overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}), \quad \underline{S}(f) = \sup_{\mathcal{P} \in \mathfrak{P}} \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P})
\end{aligned}
\] the Darboux upper integral and Darboux lower integral of \(f\) on \(R\), respectively.
If \(\overline{S}(f) = \underline{S}(f)\), then \(f\) is said to be Darboux integrable on \(R\). Their common value is denoted as \[
\begin{aligned}
I = \int_R f(x) d\mu = \int_R f(x) dx = \int_R f(x) dx_1 \dots dx_m
\end{aligned}
\] and is called the Darboux integral of \(f\) on \(R\).




Theorem 5.1 The following conclusions are equivalent:

\(f\) is Riemann integrable on the rectangle \(R\);
\(f\) is bounded and Darboux integrable on the rectangle \(R\);
\(f\) is bounded on the rectangle \(R\), and the set of discontinuities of \(f\) in \(R\) is a set of volume zero (measure zero);
For any \(\epsilon &gt; 0\), there exists a partition \(\mathcal{P}\) of the rectangle \(R\) such that \[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) - \underline{S}(f, \mathcal{P}) &lt; \epsilon.
\end{aligned}
\]

If \(f\) is Riemann (Darboux) integrable on \(R\), then the Riemann integral of \(f\) is equal to the Darboux integral of \(f\).


Theorem 5.2 Let \(R \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) be an \(m\)-dimensional rectangle. Let \(\mathscr{R}(R)\) be the set of all Riemann integrable functions on \(R\), and \(\mathscr{C}(R)\) be the set of all continuous functions on \(R\). Then:

(Linearity) \(\mathscr{R}(R)\) is a linear space, and \(\mathscr{C}(R)\) is a linear subspace of \(\mathscr{R}(R)\). Furthermore, for any \(f, g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\) and any \(\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R}\): \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_R (\alpha f(x) + \beta g(x)) d\mu = \alpha \int_R f(x) d\mu + \beta \int_R g(x) d\mu;
\end{aligned}
\]
(Monotonicity) If \(f, g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\) satisfy \(f(x) \le g(x)\) for all \(x \in R\), then: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_R f(x) d\mu \le \int_R g(x) d\mu;
\end{aligned}
\]
\(f \in \mathscr{R}(R)\) if and only if \(f_+, f_- \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), where: \[
\begin{aligned}
f_+(x) &= \max\{f(x), 0\} = \frac{|f(x)| + f(x)}{2}, \\
f_-(x) &= \max\{-f(x), 0\} = \frac{|f(x)| - f(x)}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\] Thus \(f = f_+ - f_-\) and \(|f| = f_+ + f_-\). Furthermore: \[
\begin{aligned}
\left| \int_R f(x) d\mu \right| \le \int_R |f(x)| d\mu;
\end{aligned}
\]
(Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality) For any \(f, g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), it follows that \(fg \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), and: \[
\begin{aligned}
\left| \int_R f(x)g(x) d\mu \right| \le \sqrt{\int_R |f(x)|^2 d\mu \cdot \int_R |g(x)|^2 d\mu};
\end{aligned}
\]
(Mean Value Theorem for Integrals) If \(f \in \mathscr{C}(R)\), \(g \in \mathscr{R}(R)\), and \(g(x) \ge 0\) for all \(x \in R\), then there exists \(\eta \in R\) such that:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_R f(x)g(x) d\mu = f(\eta) \int_R g(x) d\mu.
\end{aligned}
\]

5.2 Jordan Measurable Sets and Riemann Integrals






TipRiemann Integrable





Let \(D \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) be a non-empty bounded subset. For a function \(f : D \to \mathbb{R}\), we choose a rectangle \(R \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) that covers \(D\) (i.e., \(D \subseteq R\)), and define \[
\begin{aligned}
\tilde{f}(x) = \begin{cases} f(x), & x \in D; \\ 0, & x \in R \setminus D, \end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\] If \(\tilde{f}\) is Riemann integrable on \(R\), then we say \(f\) is Riemann integrable on \(D\), and denote \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_D f(x) d\mu = \int_R \tilde{f}(x) d\mu.
\end{aligned}
\]









TipJordan Measurable Set





A set \(D \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) is called a Jordan measurable set if the boundary set \(\partial D\) of \(D\) is a set of volume zero.
If \(D\) is a bounded Jordan measurable set, we denote \[
\begin{aligned}
\mu(D) = \int_D 1_D(x) d\mu,
\end{aligned}
\] which is called the \(m\)-dimensional volume of \(D\).




Theorem 5.3 Let \(D_1 \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) be a bounded closed Jordan measurable set, and \(a, b : D_1 \to \mathbb{R}\) be continuous functions. Then \[
\begin{aligned}
D = \{(\mathbf{x}, y) \in \mathbb{R}^{m+1} \mid \mathbf{x} \in D_1, a(\mathbf{x}) \le y \le b(\mathbf{x})\}
\end{aligned}
\] is a bounded closed Jordan measurable set in \(\mathbb{R}^{m+1}\).


Theorem 5.4 Let \(D \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) be a bounded Jordan measurable set. Then:

A function \(f : D \to \mathbb{R}\) is Riemann integrable if and only if \(f\) is bounded and the set of discontinuities of \(f\) is a set of volume zero.
If \(D\) is also a closed set, then all continuous functions on \(D\) are Riemann integrable functions.



Theorem 5.5 If the function \(f\) is Riemann integrable on the bounded Jordan measurable sets \(D_1\) and \(D_2\), and \(D_1 \cap D_2 \subseteq \partial D_1 \cup \partial D_2\), then \(f\) is also Riemann integrable on \(D_1 \cup D_2\), and \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{D_1 \cup D_2} f(x) d\mu = \int_{D_1} f(x) d\mu + \int_{D_2} f(x) d\mu.
\end{aligned}
\]

5.3 Calculation of Multiple Integrals

Theorem 5.6 If \(f : [a, b] \times [\alpha, \beta] \to \mathbb{R}\) is a continuous function, then \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{[a, b] \times [\alpha, \beta]} f(x, y) dx dy = \int_{\alpha}^{\beta} \left( \int_{a}^{b} f(x, y) dx \right) dy = \int_{a}^{b} \left( \int_{\alpha}^{\beta} f(x, y) dy \right) dx.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 5.7 Let \(R_1, R_2\) be rectangles in \(\mathbb{R}^m\) and \(\mathbb{R}^n\), respectively. Let \(f : R_1 \times R_2 \to \mathbb{R}\) be a Riemann integrable function. Then for any \(y \in R_2\), the Darboux upper integral and Darboux lower integral of \(f(x, y)\) with respect to \(x\): \[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{\int_{R_1}} f(x, y) dx, \quad \underline{\int_{R_1}} f(x, y) dx
\end{aligned}
\] are both Riemann integrable functions (with respect to \(y\)), and \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{R_1 \times R_2} f(x, y) dx dy = \int_{R_2} \left( \overline{\int_{R_1}} f(x, y) dx \right) dy = \int_{R_2} \left( \underline{\int_{R_1}} f(x, y) dx \right) dy.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 5.8 If \(D \subset \mathbb{R}^{m+n}\) is a bounded closed Jordan measurable set, then: \[
\begin{aligned}
D_2 = \{y \in \mathbb{R}^n \mid \exists x \in \mathbb{R}^m \text{ s.t. } (x, y) \in D\}
\end{aligned}
\]
is a bounded closed Jordan measurable set in \(\mathbb{R}^n\), and for almost all \(y \in D_2\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
D_y = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^m \mid (x, y) \in D\}
\end{aligned}
\]
is a bounded closed Jordan measurable set in \(\mathbb{R}^m\). For any continuous function \(f : D \to \mathbb{R}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_D f(x, y) dx dy = \int_{D_2} \left( \int_{D_y} f(x, y) dx \right) dy.
\end{aligned}
\]

5.4 Calculation of Multiple Integrals: Change of Variables Formula

Theorem 5.9 Let \(\tilde{\Omega} \subset \mathbb{R}^m\) be a bounded closed Jordan measurable set, and \(\varphi : \tilde{\Omega} \to \Omega = \varphi(\tilde{\Omega}) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m\) be a \(\mathscr{C}^1\) diffeomorphism. Then for any \(f \in \mathscr{R}(\Omega)\), \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\Omega} f(u) d\mu_{\Omega} = \int_{\tilde{\Omega}} f(\varphi(x)) |\det D\varphi(x)| d\mu_{\tilde{\Omega}}.
\end{aligned}
\] In traditional notation, the above equation is expressed as: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\Omega} f(u) du_1 \dots du_m = \int_{\tilde{\Omega}} f(\varphi(x)) \left| \det \frac{\partial(u_1, \dots, u_m)}{\partial(x_1, \dots, x_m)} \right| dx_1 \dots dx_m.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 5.10 Let \(\Sigma\) be an \(m \times m\) symmetric positive-definite matrix, and let \(\mu \in \mathbb{R}^m\). The integral of the multivariate Gaussian density function over the entire space \(\mathbb{R}^m\) is equal to 1: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\mathbb{R}^m} \frac{1}{\sqrt{(2\pi)^m \det \Sigma}} e^{-\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{x}-\mu)^T \Sigma^{-1}(\mathbf{x}-\mu)} dx_1 \dots dx_m = 1.
\end{aligned}
\]

TipDefinition





We call \(\mathbf{x}_0\) an interior point of a set \(E \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m\) (we also say \(E\) is a neighborhood of \(\mathbf{x}_0\) in \(\mathbb{R}^m\)) if there exists a positive number \(\delta_{\mathbf{x}_0} &gt; 0\) such that \[
\begin{aligned}
B(\mathbf{x}_0, \delta_{\mathbf{x}_0}) \subseteq E.
\end{aligned}
\]
In other words, \(\mathbf{x}_0\) and all points in its immediate vicinity are contained within \(E\). The set formed by all interior points of \(E\) is called the interior of \(E\), denoted as \(\text{int} E\).
We call \(E\) an open set if \(E = \text{int} E\). This implies that every point in \(E\) is an interior point, or that \(E\) is an open neighborhood for each of its members.









TipDifferential





Let \(\mathbf{x}_0\) be an interior point of a set \(E \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m\). We say that \(f: E \to \mathbb{R}^n\) is differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\) if there exists a linear mapping \(A: \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}^n\) such that: \[
\begin{aligned}
f(\mathbf{x}_0 + \mathbf{v}) = f(\mathbf{x}_0) + A\mathbf{v} + o(\|\mathbf{v}\|), \quad \mathbf{v} \to \mathbf{0}.
\end{aligned}
\]
That also means: \[
\begin{aligned}
\|f(\mathbf{x}_0 + \mathbf{v}) - f(\mathbf{x}_0) - A\mathbf{v} \| = o(\|\mathbf{v}\|), \quad \mathbf{v} \to \mathbf{0}.
\end{aligned}
\]
In this case, we call the linear mapping \(A\) the differential of \(f\) at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), denoted as: \[
\begin{aligned}
df(\mathbf{x}_0): \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}^n, \quad df(\mathbf{x}_0)(\mathbf{v}) = A\mathbf{v}.
\end{aligned}
\]




Theorem 2.1 If \(f\) is differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), then \(f\) is continuous at \(\mathbf{x}_0\).







TipDirectional Derivative





Let \(f: E \to \mathbb{R}^n\), \(\mathbf{x}_0 \in E\), and a vector \(\mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^m\) satisfy the following: there exists \(\delta &gt; 0\) such that for any \(0 \le t &lt; \delta\), we have \(\mathbf{x}_0 + t\mathbf{v} \in E\).
If the limit \[
\begin{aligned}
\left. \frac{d}{dt} (f(\mathbf{x}_0 + t\mathbf{v})) \right|_{t=0} = \lim_{t \to 0} \frac{f(\mathbf{x}_0 + t\mathbf{v}) - f(\mathbf{x}_0)}{t}
\end{aligned}
\] exists, then we denote it as \(\frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x}_0)\) or \(\partial_{\mathbf{v}}f(\mathbf{x}_0)\), and call it the derivative of \(f\) at \(\mathbf{x}_0\) along vector \(\mathbf{v}\).
In particular, when \(\mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^m\) is a unit vector (i.e., \(\|\mathbf{v}\| = 1\)), we call it the directional derivative of \(f\) at \(\mathbf{x}_0\) along direction \(\mathbf{v}\). \[
\begin{aligned} \frac{df}{ds} = \lim_{\Delta s \to 0} \frac{\Delta f}{\Delta s} \end{aligned}
\]




Theorem 2.2 If \(f\) is differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), then \(f\) has a derivative at \(\mathbf{x}_0\) along every vector \(\mathbf{v}\), and: \[
\begin{aligned}
df(\mathbf{x}_0)(\mathbf{v}) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x}_0).
\end{aligned}
\]
Furthermore, in this case, the derivative \(\frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x}_0)\) is linear with respect to the vector \(\mathbf{v}\).


Theorem 2.3  

Any bilinear mapping \(B: \mathbb{R}^m \times \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^p\) is differentiable. For any point \((\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{y}_0)\) and increment \((\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v})\), the differential is:

\[
\begin{aligned}
dB(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{y}_0)(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}) = B(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{v}) + B(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{y}_0).
\end{aligned}
\]

Any multilinear mapping \(L: \mathbb{R}^{m_1} \times \mathbb{R}^{m_2} \times \dots \times \mathbb{R}^{m_k} \to \mathbb{R}^p\) is differentiable, with the differential given by:

\[
\begin{aligned}
dL(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2, \dots, \mathbf{x}_k)(\mathbf{u}_1, \mathbf{u}_2, \dots, \mathbf{u}_k) = \sum_{j=1}^k L(\mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{u}_j, \dots, \mathbf{x}_k).
\end{aligned}
\]

The determinant \(\det A\) of an \(n \times n\) square matrix \(A\) is a multilinear function of its column vectors \(\mathbf{a}_1, \dots, \mathbf{a}_n\). Therefore, the determinant is a differentiable function:

\[
\begin{aligned}
d\det(A)(B) = \sum_{j=1}^n \det(\mathbf{a}_1, \dots, \mathbf{b}_j, \dots, \mathbf{a}_n) = \sum_{j=1}^n \sum_{i=1}^n b_j^i A^{*i}_j = \text{tr}(A^{*T} B).
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 2.4 Let \(A_0 \in \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n)\) be an invertible matrix, and define the open set \(U\) as: \[
\begin{aligned}
U = \left\{ A \in \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n) \mid \|A - A_0\| &lt; \frac{1}{\|A_0^{-1}\|} \right\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The inversion mapping \(f\) is differentiable on \(U\). \[
\begin{aligned}
f: U \to \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n), \quad f(A) = A^{-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]
and \[
\begin{aligned}
df(A)(B) = -A^{-1} B A^{-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 2.5 (Chain Rule for Derivatives of Composite Functions) Let \(f\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), and let \(g\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{y}_0 = f(\mathbf{x}_0)\). Then the composite function \(g \circ f\) is differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), and the differential of the composite is equal to the composite of the differentials: \[
\begin{aligned}
d(g \circ f)(\mathbf{x}_0) = dg(\mathbf{y}_0) \circ df(\mathbf{x}_0) = dg(f(\mathbf{x}_0)) \circ df(\mathbf{x}_0).
\end{aligned}
\]

