\end{aligned}
\]

The determinant \(\det A\) of an \(n \times n\) square matrix \(A\) is a multilinear function of its column vectors \(\mathbf{a}_1, \dots, \mathbf{a}_n\). Therefore, the determinant is a differentiable function:

\[
\begin{aligned}
d\det(A)(B) = \sum_{j=1}^n \det(\mathbf{a}_1, \dots, \mathbf{b}_j, \dots, \mathbf{a}_n) = \sum_{j=1}^n \sum_{i=1}^n b_j^i A^{*i}_j = \text{tr}(A^{*T} B).
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 2.4 Let \(A_0 \in \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n)\) be an invertible matrix, and define the open set \(U\) as: \[
\begin{aligned}
U = \left\{ A \in \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n) \mid \|A - A_0\| &lt; \frac{1}{\|A_0^{-1}\|} \right\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The inversion mapping \(f\) is differentiable on \(U\). \[
\begin{aligned}
f: U \to \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n), \quad f(A) = A^{-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]
and \[
\begin{aligned}
df(A)(B) = -A^{-1} B A^{-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 2.5 (Chain Rule for Derivatives of Composite Functions) Let \(f\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), and let \(g\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{y}_0 = f(\mathbf{x}_0)\). Then the composite function \(g \circ f\) is differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), and the differential of the composite is equal to the composite of the differentials: \[
\begin{aligned}
d(g \circ f)(\mathbf{x}_0) = dg(\mathbf{y}_0) \circ df(\mathbf{x}_0) = dg(f(\mathbf{x}_0)) \circ df(\mathbf{x}_0).
\end{aligned}
\]

2.2 Coordinate Systems and Partial Derivatives






TipPartial Derivative





Let \((x^1, x^2, \dots, x^m)\) be the coordinates of a point in \(E\). Suppose that at an interior point \(\mathbf{x}_0 = (a^1, \dots, a^m)\) of \(E\), the \(m\)-variable function \(f: E \to \mathbb{R}\) has the following limit: \[
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{t \to 0} \frac{f(a^1, \dots, a^k + t, \dots, a^m) - f(a^1, \dots, a^k, \dots, a^m)}{t},
\end{aligned}
\]
Then the value of this limit is denoted by \(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x^k}(\mathbf{x}_0)\), \(f_{x^k}(\mathbf{x}_0)\) (written as \(f'_{x^k}(\mathbf{x}_0)\) in classical textbooks), \(\partial_{x^k} f(\mathbf{x}_0)\), or \(\partial_k f(\mathbf{x}_0)\), and is called the partial derivative of \(f\) with respect to the coordinate \(x^k\).




Theorem 2.6 Let the \(m\)-variable function \(f\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\). Then for any \(\mathbf{v} = (\xi^1, \dots, \xi^m)^T \in \mathbb{R}^m\), \[
\begin{aligned}
df(\mathbf{x}_0)(\mathbf{v}) &= \frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x}_0) = \xi^1 \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}_0) + \dots + \xi^m \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}_0) \\
&= \left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}_0), \dots, \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}_0) \right) \begin{pmatrix} \xi^1 \\ \vdots \\ \xi^m \end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore, the differential of the function \(f\) is \[
\begin{aligned}
df(\mathbf{x}_0) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}_0) dx^1 + \dots + \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}_0) dx^m,
\end{aligned}
\]
where \[
\begin{aligned}
dx^i : \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}, \quad dx^i(\mathbf{v}) = \xi^i,
\end{aligned}
\] is the differential of the coordinate function \(x^i : \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}, \quad (x^1, \dots, x^m) \mapsto x^i\); they are the coordinate functions of the vector.







TipJacobian Matrix





If the \(m\)-variable mapping \(F: E \to \mathbb{R}^n\), \[
\begin{aligned}
F(x^1, \dots, x^m) = (f^1(x^1, \dots, x^m), \dots, f^n(x^1, \dots, x^m))^T
\end{aligned}
\] is differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), then for any \(\mathbf{v} = (\xi^1, \dots, \xi^m) \in \mathbb{R}^m\), \[
\begin{aligned}
dF(\mathbf{x}_0)(\mathbf{v}) = \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\partial f^1}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}_0) & \cdots & \frac{\partial f^1}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}_0) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
\frac{\partial f^n}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}_0) & \cdots & \frac{\partial f^n}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}_0)
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\xi^1 \\
\vdots \\
\xi^m
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore, the coordinate representation of the differential of the mapping \(F\) is \[
\begin{aligned}
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\partial f^1}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}_0) & \cdots & \frac{\partial f^1}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}_0) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
\frac{\partial f^n}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}_0) & \cdots & \frac{\partial f^n}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}_0)
\end{pmatrix},
\end{aligned}
\]
Denote this matrix by \(JF(\mathbf{x}_0) = \left. \frac{\partial(y^1, \dots, y^n)}{\partial(x^1, \dots, x^m)} \right|_{\mathbf{x}_0}\), called the Jacobian matrix of \(F\) at \(\mathbf{x}_0\). The determinant of \(JF(\mathbf{x}_0)\), \(\det JF(\mathbf{x}_0)\), is called the Jacobian determinant of \(F\) at \(\mathbf{x}_0\).




Theorem 2.7 Let \(G\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\), and \(F\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{y}_0 = G(\mathbf{x}_0)\), then \[
\begin{aligned}
J(F \circ G)(\mathbf{x}_0) = JF(\mathbf{y}_0) \cdot JG(\mathbf{x}_0).
\end{aligned}
\] If the inverse mapping \(F^{-1}\) of a differentiable mapping \(F\) is also differentiable, then \[
\begin{aligned}
J(F^{-1})(\mathbf{y}_0) = (JF(\mathbf{x}_0))^{-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 2.8 If all first-order partial derivatives \(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}), \dots, \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x})\) are continuous, then \(f\) is differentiable. (It is not a necessary condition.)

2.3 Gradient and Directional Derivative

Theorem 2.9 Let \(\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle\) be an inner product on \(\mathbb{R}^m\). Then for any linear function \(L: \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}\) on \(\mathbb{R}^m\), there exists a unique vector \(\nabla L \in \mathbb{R}^m\) such that \[
\begin{aligned}
L(\mathbf{v}) = \langle \mathbf{v}, \nabla L \rangle, \quad \forall \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^m.
\end{aligned}
\]
Consequently, \(\nabla L\) is orthogonal to \(\operatorname{Ker} L\), and \(\|\nabla L\| = \|L\| = \max_{\|\mathbf{v}\|=1} L(\mathbf{v})\). A unit vector \(\mathbf{v}\) satisfies \(L(\mathbf{v}) = \|\nabla L\|\) if and only if \(\mathbf{v}\) is the unit vector in the direction of \(\nabla L\). This unique vector \(\nabla L\) is called the gradient vector of the linear function \(L\).







TipGradient Vector





Let \(E\) be a subset of an inner product space, and the function \(f : E \to \mathbb{R}\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\). Denote the gradient vector of \(df(\mathbf{x}_0)\) as \(\operatorname{grad} f(\mathbf{x}_0)\) or \(\nabla f(\mathbf{x}_0)\), called the gradient vector of \(f\) at \(\mathbf{x}_0\). Thus, \[
\begin{aligned}
df(\mathbf{x}_0)(\mathbf{v}) = \langle \mathbf{v}, \nabla f(\mathbf{x}_0) \rangle.
\end{aligned}
\]




Theorem 2.10 Let \(f\) be differentiable at \(\mathbf{x}_0\). Then for any vector \(\mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^m\), \[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x}_0) = df(\mathbf{x}_0)(\mathbf{v}) = \langle \mathbf{v}, \nabla f(\mathbf{x}_0) \rangle.
\end{aligned}
\]
Consequently, for any unit vector \(\mathbf{v}\), \[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{v}}(\mathbf{x}_0) \le \|\nabla f(\mathbf{x}_0)\|,
\end{aligned}
\] where equality holds if and only if \(\mathbf{v}\) is in the direction of the gradient of \(f\) at \(\mathbf{x}_0\). Therefore, the gradient direction of \(f\) is the direction in which \(f\) increases most rapidly.


Theorem 2.11 In any coordinate system \((x^1, \dots, x^m)\), the differential of a differentiable function \(f\) is a row vector: \[
\begin{aligned}
df(\mathbf{x}) = \left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}), \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^2}(\mathbf{x}), \dots, \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x}) \right).
\end{aligned}
\]
In a Cartesian coordinate system \((x^1, \dots, x^m)\), the gradient of a differentiable function \(f\) is a column vector: \[
\begin{aligned}
\nabla f(\mathbf{x}) = \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x^1}(\mathbf{x}) \\
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x^2}(\mathbf{x}) \\
\vdots \\
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x^m}(\mathbf{x})
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
\]

TipArc Length of a Curve





A regular \(\mathscr{C}^1\) parameterized curve \(\mathbf{x} : [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}^m\) is defined if it is injective and, for any \(t \in [a, b]\), \(\|\mathbf{x}'(t)\| &gt; 0\). We define
\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{[a,b]} \|\mathbf{x}'(t)\| dt = \int_{a}^{b} \|\mathbf{x}'(t)\| dt.
\end{aligned}
\]
as the arc length of the regular \(\mathscr{C}^1\) parameterized curve \(\mathbf{x} : [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}^m\).









TipIntegration of a Function Along a Curve





Let \(f : \gamma \to \mathbb{R}\) be a continuous function, and let \(\mathbf{x}(t)\) be any \(\mathscr{C}^1\) parameterization of the curve \(\gamma\). Denote
\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{\gamma} f dl = \int_{[a,b]} f(\mathbf{x}(t))\|\mathbf{x}'(t)\| dt,
\end{aligned}
\]
which is called the integral of \(f\) along \(\gamma\). From the chain rule and the change of variables formula for integration, it is known that the value of the above integral is independent of the choice of the parameterization form of the curve \(\gamma\).









TipArc Length in Different Coordinate Systems





In the Euclidean rectangular coordinate system:
\[
\begin{aligned}
dl = \|\mathbf{x}'(t)\| dt = \sqrt{\langle \mathbf{x}'(t), \mathbf{x}'(t) \rangle} dt = \sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^{m} x'_k(t)^2} dt = \sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^{m} (dx_k)^2}
\end{aligned}
\]
If we consider a coordinate transformation \(\mathbf{x} = \varphi(\mathbf{u})\), then:
\[
\begin{aligned}
dl &= \|\mathbf{x}'(t)\| dt = \sqrt{\langle \mathbf{x}'(t), \mathbf{x}'(t) \rangle} dt \\
&= \sqrt{\langle \partial \varphi(\mathbf{u}(t)) \mathbf{u}'(t), \partial \varphi(\mathbf{u}(t)) \mathbf{u}'(t) \rangle} dt \\
&= \sqrt{\left\langle \sum_{i=1}^{m} \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_i}(\mathbf{u}(t)) u'_i(t), \sum_{j=1}^{m} \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_j}(\mathbf{u}(t)) u'_j(t) \right\rangle} dt \\
&= \sqrt{\sum_{i,j=1}^{m} \left\langle \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_i}(\mathbf{u}(t)), \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_j}(\mathbf{u}(t)) \right\rangle du_i du_j} \\
&= \sqrt{(du_1, \dots, du_m) \left( \left\langle \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_i}(\mathbf{u}(t)), \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_j}(\mathbf{u}(t)) \right\rangle \right)_{m \times m} \begin{pmatrix} du_1 \\ \vdots \\ du_m \end{pmatrix}}
\end{aligned}
\]
This is a form independent of coordinates and related only to the metric (i.e., the inner product). It is applicable to various coordinate systems \((u_1, \dots, u_m)\). The matrix
\[
\begin{aligned}
\left( \left\langle \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_i}(\mathbf{u}), \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}}{\partial u_j}(\mathbf{u}) \right\rangle \right)_{m \times m}
\end{aligned}
\]
is called the Metric Matrix or the Gram Matrix. In particular, when \(\partial \varphi(\mathbf{u})\) is an orthogonal transformation, the metric matrix becomes the identity matrix. Consequently, the infinitesimal arc length in the coordinate system \((u_1, \dots, u_m)\) is equal to the infinitesimal arc length in the coordinate system \((x_1, \dots, x_m)\).

