TipCalculus of Variations





The above problem can be summarized as: \[
\begin{aligned}
\min \int_{a}^{b} L(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) dt, \quad \mathbf{x}(a) = A, \mathbf{x}(b) = B.
\end{aligned}
\]
\(L(t, \mathbf{x}, \dot{\mathbf{x}})\): Lagrangian function
Embed the curve \(\mathbf{x}(t)\) to be solved into a family of curves \(X(t, s)\) (where for each fixed \(s\), \(X(t, s)\) is a curve) such that: \[
\begin{aligned}
X(t, 0) = \mathbf{x}(t), \quad \forall t,
\end{aligned}
\] and for all \(s\), we have \(X(a, s) = A\) and \(X(b, s) = B\). For example: \(X(t, s) = \mathbf{x}(t) + s\mathbf{y}(t)\), where \(\mathbf{y}(a) = \mathbf{y}(b) = 0\).
Consider: \[
\begin{aligned}
F(s) = \int_{a}^{b} L\left(t, X(t, s), \frac{\partial X}{\partial t}(t, s)\right) dt.
\end{aligned}
\] Then \(F(0) = \int_{a}^{b} L(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) dt\) is the minimum value.
Thus \(F'(0) = 0\). Namely, \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{a}^{b} \left[ L_{\mathbf{x}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) X_s(t, 0) + L_{\dot{\mathbf{x}}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) X_{s,t}(t, 0) \right] dt = 0
\end{aligned}
\]
By integration by parts, the second term becomes: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{a}^{b} L_{\dot{\mathbf{x}}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) d_t X_s(t, 0) &= \left. L_{\dot{\mathbf{x}}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) X_s(t, 0) \right|_{a}^{b} - \int_{a}^{b} \frac{d}{dt} L_{\dot{\mathbf{x}}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) X_s(t, 0) dt \\
&= - \int_{a}^{b} \frac{d}{dt} L_{\dot{\mathbf{x}}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) X_s(t, 0) dt
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore, \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{a}^{b} \left[ L_{\mathbf{x}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) - \frac{d}{dt} L_{\dot{\mathbf{x}}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) \right] X_s(t, 0) dt = 0
\end{aligned}
\]
Since \(X_s(t, 0)\) is arbitrary, we obtain the Euler-Lagrange equation: \[
\begin{aligned}
L_{\mathbf{x}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) - \frac{d}{dt} L_{\dot{\mathbf{x}}}(t, \mathbf{x}(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)) = 0.
\end{aligned}
\] This is a second-order ordinary differential equation (ODE) with respect to \(\mathbf{x}(t)\).
Reducible Case 1: \(L = L(\mathbf{x}, \dot{\mathbf{x}})\):
The Euler-Lagrange equation is: \[
\begin{aligned}
L_x(x, \dot{x}) - \frac{d}{dt} L_{\dot{x}}(x, \dot{x}) = 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
Multiplying by \(\dot{x}\) and using the chain rule: \[
\begin{aligned}
L_x(x, \dot{x})\dot{x} + L_{\dot{x}}(x, \dot{x})\ddot{x} - \frac{d}{dt} \left( L_{\dot{x}}(x, \dot{x})\dot{x} \right) = 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
Since \(\frac{d}{dt} L(x, \dot{x}) = L_x\dot{x} + L_{\dot{x}}\ddot{x}\) when \(L\) does not explicitly depend on \(t\), we have: \[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dt} \left( L(x, \dot{x}) - L_{\dot{x}}(x, \dot{x})\dot{x} \right) = 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
Which leads to the Beltrami Identity: \[
\begin{aligned}
L(x, \dot{x}) - L_{\dot{x}}(x, \dot{x})\dot{x} = C.
\end{aligned}
\]

4.2 Improper Integrals with Parameters
For a function \(f : [a, +\infty) \times U \to \mathbb{R}\) (where \(U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n\)), the integral \(\int_{a}^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) dx\) is called an improper integral depending on a parameter \(\mathbf{y}\).
A convergent improper integral with a parameter defines a function with \(\mathbf{y}\) as the independent variable: \[
\begin{aligned}
F(\mathbf{y}) = \int_{a}^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) dx.
\end{aligned}
\]






TipDefinition





The improper integral \(\int_{a}^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) dx\) is said to converge uniformly to \(F(\mathbf{y})\) on \(U\) if for every \(\epsilon &gt; 0\), there exists \(A(\epsilon) &gt; a\) such that for all \(A &gt; A(\epsilon)\) and for every \(\mathbf{y} \in U\), we have: \[
\begin{aligned}
\left| \int_{a}^{A} f(x, \mathbf{y}) dx - F(\mathbf{y}) \right| &lt; \epsilon.
\end{aligned}
\]




Theorem 4.6 Let the function \(f(x, \mathbf{y})\) be Riemann integrable with respect to \(x\) on any bounded interval \(I \subset [a, +\infty)\). The improper integral \(\int_{a}^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) dx\) converges uniformly with respect to \(\mathbf{y} \in U\) if and only if it satisfies the uniform Cauchy condition: for any \(\epsilon &gt; 0\), there exists \(A(\epsilon) &gt; a\) such that \[
\begin{aligned}
\left| \int_{A_1}^{A_2} f(x, \mathbf{y}) dx \right| &lt; \epsilon, \quad \forall A_2 &gt; A_1 \ge A(\epsilon), \quad \forall \mathbf{y} \in U.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 4.7 Let the functions \(f(x, \mathbf{y})\) and \(g(x, \mathbf{y})\) satisfy: \[
\begin{aligned}
|f(x, \mathbf{y})| \le g(x, \mathbf{y}), \quad \forall x \ge a, \forall \mathbf{y} \in U
\end{aligned}
\] If the integral \(\int_{a}^{+\infty} g(x, \mathbf{y}) dx\) converges uniformly with respect to \(\mathbf{y} \in U\), then the integral \(\int_{a}^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) dx\) converges uniformly (and absolutely) with respect to \(\mathbf{y} \in U\).


Theorem 4.8 (Dirichlet and Abel Tests) Assume the finite-interval integrals exist. The improper integral \(\int_a^\infty f(x,y)g(x,y)\,dx\) converges uniformly for \(y\in U\) under either set of conditions:

Dirichlet’s test:

\(F(t,y)=\int_a^t f(x,y)\,dx\) is bounded uniformly in \(t\ge a\) and \(y\in U\).
For each \(y\), \(g(x,y)\) is monotone in \(x\).
\(g(x,y)\to0\) as \(x\to\infty\), uniformly for \(y\in U\).

Abel’s test:

\(\int_a^\infty f(x,y)\,dx\) converges uniformly for \(y\in U\).
For each \(y\), \(g(x,y)\) is monotone in \(x\).
\(g(x,y)\) is bounded uniformly in \(x\ge a\) and \(y\in U\).




Theorem 4.9 Let the function \(f: [a, +\infty) \times U \to \mathbb{R} \ (U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m)\) satisfy:

The improper integral \(F(\mathbf{y}) = \int_a^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) \text{d}x\) converges uniformly on \(U\);
For any \(x \ge a\), \(f(x, \mathbf{y})\) is continuous with respect to \(\mathbf{y}\) at \(\mathbf{y}_0 \in U\), and this continuity is uniform with respect to \(x\) in any bounded closed interval \([a, b]\): that is, for any \(\epsilon &gt; 0\), there exists \(\delta(\mathbf{y}_0, \epsilon) &gt; 0\), such that for any \(x \in [a, b]\), as long as \(\mathbf{y} \in U\) satisfies \(\|\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{y}_0\| &lt; \delta(\mathbf{y}_0, \epsilon)\), we have \(|f(x, \mathbf{y}) - f(x, \mathbf{y}_0)| &lt; \epsilon\).

Then \(F\) is continuous at \(\mathbf{y}_0\), i.e., \[
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{\mathbf{y} \to \mathbf{y}_0} \int_a^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) \text{d}x = \int_a^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}_0) \text{d}x = \int_a^{+\infty} \lim_{\mathbf{y} \to \mathbf{y}_0} f(x, \mathbf{y}) \text{d}x.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 4.10 Let the continuous function of two variables \(f: [a, +\infty) \times [1, +\infty) \to \mathbb{R}\) satisfy:

The improper integral \(F(y) = \int_a^{+\infty} f(x, y) \text{d}x\) converges uniformly on \([1, +\infty)\);
For any \(x \ge a\), the limit \(\lim_{y \to +\infty} f(x, y) = g(x)\) exists, and this limit is uniform with respect to \(x\) in any bounded closed interval \([a, b]\): that is, for any \(\epsilon &gt; 0\) and any interval \([a, b]\), there exists \(N_{a,b,\epsilon} &gt; 1\), such that for any \(x \in [a, b]\), whenever \(y &gt; N_{a,b,\epsilon}\), we have \(|f(x, y) - g(x)| &lt; \epsilon\).

Then \[
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{y \to +\infty} \int_a^{+\infty} f(x, y) \text{d}x = \int_a^{+\infty} g(x) \text{d}x = \int_a^{+\infty} \lim_{y \to +\infty} f(x, y) \text{d}x.
\end{aligned}
\]


