The improper integral \(\int_a^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}_0) \text{d}x\) converges;
For each \(k = 1, 2, \dots, m\), \(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_k}(x, \mathbf{y})\) is continuous, and the improper integral \(\int_a^{+\infty} \frac{\partial f}{\partial y_k}(x, \mathbf{y}) \text{d}x\) converges uniformly with respect to \(\mathbf{y}\) in \(U\).

Then \(\int_a^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) \text{d}x\) converges uniformly in a neighborhood of \(\mathbf{y}_0\), is of class \(\mathscr{C}^1\) with respect to \(\mathbf{y}\), and \[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial}{\partial y_k} \int_a^{+\infty} f(x, \mathbf{y}) \text{d}x = \int_a^{+\infty} \frac{\partial f}{\partial y_k}(x, \mathbf{y}) \text{d}x, \quad k = 1, 2, \dots, m.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 4.12 Let \(f : [c, +\infty) \times [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}\) be continuous, and the improper integral \(\int_c^{+\infty} f(x, y)dx\) converges uniformly with respect to \(y\) on \([a, b]\). Then the improper integral \(\int_c^{+\infty} \int_a^b f(x, y)dydx\) converges, and \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_a^b \int_c^{+\infty} f(x, y)dxdy = \int_c^{+\infty} \int_a^b f(x, y)dydx.
\end{aligned}
\]


Theorem 4.13 Let \(f: [c, +\infty) \times [a, +\infty) \to \mathbb{R}\) be continuous, satisfying:

At least one of the iterated integrals \(\int_a^{+\infty} \int_c^{+\infty} |f(x, y)| dx dy\) and \(\int_c^{+\infty} \int_a^{+\infty} |f(x, y)| dy dx\) exists.
The improper integral \(\int_c^{+\infty} f(x, y) dx\) converges uniformly with respect to \(y\) on any bounded closed interval \([a, b]\).
The improper integral \(\int_a^{+\infty} f(x, y) dy\) converges uniformly with respect to \(x\) on any bounded closed interval \([c, d]\).

Then \[
\begin{aligned}
\int_a^{+\infty} \int_c^{+\infty} f(x, y) dx dy = \int_c^{+\infty} \int_a^{+\infty} f(x, y) dy dx.
\end{aligned}
\]







TipGamma Function





\[
\begin{aligned}
\Gamma(\alpha) = \int_{0}^{+\infty} x^{\alpha-1} \mathrm{e}^{-x} \mathrm{d}x
\end{aligned}
\]
It is a \(\mathscr{C}^\infty\) function for \(\alpha &gt; 0\), satisfying:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Gamma(1) &= 1 \\
\Gamma(\alpha+1) &= \alpha\Gamma(\alpha), \quad \forall \alpha &gt; 0
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus, the \(\Gamma\) function is a generalization of the factorial of natural numbers, embedding the factorial sequence into a \(\mathscr{C}^\infty\) function. Additionally, the \(\Gamma\) function possesses the log-convex property:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ln \Gamma\left(\frac{\alpha + \beta}{2}\right) \le \frac{\ln \Gamma(\alpha) + \ln \Gamma(\beta)}{2}, \quad \forall \alpha &gt; 0, \beta &gt; 0
\end{aligned}
\]









TipBeta Function





\[
\begin{aligned}
B(\alpha, \beta) = \int_{0}^{1} x^{\alpha-1}(1 - x)^{\beta - 1} \mathrm{d}x, \quad \alpha &gt; 0, \beta &gt; 0
\end{aligned}
\]
It is a \(\mathscr{C}^\infty\) function, satisfying:
\[
\begin{aligned}
B(\alpha, \beta) = \frac{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)}{\Gamma(\alpha + \beta)}
\end{aligned}
\]

6.2 Homogeneous Differential Equations
Equations of the form \(y' = f\left(\frac{y}{x}\right)\) are known as homogeneous differential equations. They can be solved by using a substitution that transforms them into a separable equation.
Let \(u = \frac{y}{x}\), which implies: \[
\begin{aligned}
y = ux
\end{aligned}
\]
Differentiating both sides with respect to \(x\) using the product rule, we get: \[
\begin{aligned}
y' = u + x \frac{du}{dx}
\end{aligned}
\]
Substitute \(y'\) and \(\frac{y}{x}\) back into the original equation \(y' = f\left(\frac{y}{x}\right)\): \[
\begin{aligned}
u + x \frac{du}{dx} = f(u)
\end{aligned}
\]
Rearrange the equation to isolate \(u\) and \(x\): \[
\begin{aligned}
x \frac{du}{dx} = f(u) - u
\end{aligned}
\] \[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{du}{f(u) - u} = \frac{dx}{x}
\end{aligned}
\]
Integrate both sides: \[
\begin{aligned}
\int \frac{1}{f(u) - u} du = \int \frac{1}{x} dx
\end{aligned}
\] After solving the integral, replace \(u\) with \(\frac{y}{x}\) to obtain the final general solution.
